WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.969
Compiler and linker settings influence the final binary, not just the source code.

2
00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:13.378
Release hardening includes how the executable is laid out, which sections are writable or executable, how relocations are handled, and whether the

3
00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:16.818
operating system can apply its own protection mechanisms effectively.

4
00:00:16.818 --> 00:00:24.080
Position-independent executables support address-space randomization by allowing the operating system to place the executable at varied locations.

5
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.813
This is a release-build property that depends on compiler, linker, platform, and packaging choices.

6
00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:35.164
Teams should verify it in the delivered artifact, not only in the build script.

7
00:00:35.164 --> 00:00:38.604
Memory permission hardening aims to reduce unnecessary executable regions.

8
00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:45.867
For example, a stack or data region should not be executable unless a platform-specific design explicitly requires it.

9
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:51.982
Linker options and platform defaults can influence these properties, so release review should include binary inspection.

10
00:00:51.982 --> 00:00:56.951
Relocation hardening concepts focus on making dynamic linking behavior less permissive after startup.

11
00:00:56.951 --> 00:01:04.978
The exact options vary across platforms and toolchains, but the security intent is consistent: reduce mutable runtime surfaces where the platform

12
00:01:04.978 --> 00:01:06.124
supports doing so.

13
00:01:06.124 --> 00:01:08.036
The defensive lesson is verification.

14
00:01:08.036 --> 00:01:12.240
A CMake option, project property, or wiki entry is not enough.

15
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.973
Review the compiler commands, linker commands, CI build logs, packaging process, and final binary properties.

16
00:01:17.973 --> 00:01:22.560
The artifact that ships is the thing that needs to match policy.
